      "math_academy_id": "q-42996",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],$ find $det(A).$",
      "worked_solution": "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that both matrices are triangular. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =(5\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 2)\\cdot (3\\cdot 2\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =(-10)\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =120.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
